<article class="detail-page"><a class="detail-back" href="/insights/">← Back to insights</a><div class="section-label">Operations & SOPs</div><h1>Your Business Shouldn't Depend on You. Here's How to Write SOPs That Actually Get Used</h1><div class="detail-content modal-body"><p>Most SOPs fail not because they are badly written — but because they are written for the wrong audience, stored in the wrong place, and never reinforced. Here is how to fix all three.</p><h4>Write for the Person Doing the Job</h4><p>An SOP written by a manager for a manager will not be followed by the person on the floor. Every SOP should be written at the reading level of the person who will use it — with photos, screenshots, or diagrams wherever possible. If it requires more than 10 seconds to understand a step, rewrite the step.</p><h4>The Right Structure for Every SOP</h4><p>Every effective SOP has five sections: (1) Purpose — why this process matters. (2) Scope — who this applies to. (3) Steps — numbered, sequential, unambiguous. (4) Exceptions — what to do when things go wrong. (5) Owner — who is responsible for maintaining this SOP.</p><h4>Where SOPs Go to Die</h4><p>SOPs stored in a shared drive folder that nobody visits will not be followed. The best SOPs we have seen are printed and laminated at the workstation, or embedded directly into the software system used for that task. If it is not visible at the moment of use, it does not exist.</p><h4>Review Cycles</h4><p>Every SOP should have a quarterly review date. A process that is not reviewed becomes outdated within 6 months in a growing business. Assign SOP ownership to a specific role — not a person — and make review a standing agenda item in your operational review meetings.</p></div><a class="btn-primary" href="/#contact">Request a Discovery Call →</a></article>
